Abstract: This paper proposes that an annotation be invoked at compile time for each construct it applies to, a declaration or a base-specifier, whenever the type of its value declares a callee with a `std::meta::annotation_context` parameter.
Abstract:
Abstract: A C++26 annotation cannot observe the construct it applies to. A check on where it may be applied is therefore written outside the annotation and reports a violation somewhere other than at that construct. A `consteval` block can run such a check, but only in code whose author remembers to write one. Declaring the parameter is the opt-in, which EWG is asked to weigh against the validity of a direct call. Because the parameter type is new, every C++26 annotation keeps its behavior. An annotation that opts in cannot fall back to passive metadata, since a call that cannot be formed is an error. The invocation is a mandatory constant evaluation that can reject the construct but changes neither it nor the annotation. A separately polled request, needed only for reflection-driven synthesis, would also let an invocation inject declarations where a `consteval` block at the annotation's position could.
Boilerplate: omit conformance, omit feedback-header
Markup Shorthands: markdown yes
