</tbody>
</table>

Scheduling and the injection integration still need Core review.

## A new macro detects the facility ## {#wording-impact-macro}

[[P3394R4]] proposes no macro of its own and [cpp.predefined] carries no `__cpp_annotations`. Annotations shipped under the reflection macros. An implementation can ship reflection and annotations without this facility, which leaves a user testing for it with a question `__cpp_impl_reflection` cannot answer. We therefore propose a new macro, `__cpp_active_annotations` in [cpp.predefined], for the invocation rule, with `__cpp_lib_reflection` in [version.syn] updated for `annotation_context`. Both values are left to the plenary that adopts this proposal.

# Scheduling is the principal implementation risk # {#implementability}

No implementation of active annotations is reported here. Beyond annotation formation ([dcl.attr.annotation]), the annotation queries ([meta.reflection.annotation]), and constant evaluation ([expr.const]), the added work is deciding the opt-in from the value's type, forming the call from the underlying constant with the specified access anchor, constructing the context prvalue, assigning the processing point, and enforcing the required evaluation and its diagnostics. Scheduling is the principal risk, through its interaction with complete-class contexts, templates, declaration reachability, and the ordering of injected declarations. Those interactions need a prototype.

Template specializations and injected annotations multiply the invocations, while deciding that an annotation is passive reads only its value's type. Implementations may cache or reuse work when doing so preserves the required identity, evaluation context, diagnostics, and other observable effects. There are no compile-time performance measurements for this proposal.

A prototype's test plan starts with the declared-parameter forms: static and non-static call operators, explicit object member functions, captureless lambdas, function pointers, and surrogate call functions, each taking the parameter by value, by `const` reference, and by rvalue reference, beside a callee taking `auto` that stays passive. Negative tests check that each near miss is an error ([[#semantics-detection|the opt-in rule]]), as is a null pointer whose function type declares the parameter. The plan also covers multiple annotations on one target, one occurrence applying to more than one target, in-class and nested-class annotations, function definitions, lambda parameters, default arguments, explicit and implicit template instantiation, modules, exception propagation, required destruction of discarded results, and the nested invocations of injected members and of specializations first required during a constant evaluation.

# Objections on implementation, termination, and injection are answered # {#objections}

Each objection below is answered here rather than deferred to [[#open-questions|the open questions]]. Where the answer is partial, it says so.

## "Without an implementation, nobody knows this is implementable" ## {#objections-implementation}

Correct ([[#implementability|implementability]]). The opt-in reads only the value's type and the invocation reuses expression semantics every implementation already has. The risk is in scheduling, which adds evaluation points inside the frontend's processing of declarations. A direction poll taken before a prototype is a poll on the model rather than on its cost.

## "The design admits a translation that does not terminate" ## {#objections-termination}

Partly accepted. Chains of invocations run nested and fall under the limits that already apply ([[#timing-templates|specializations]], [[#timing-injected|injected members]]). A chain of injections nests each invocation in the evaluation that injected its class and is an error once an evaluation in the nest exceeds the implementation's limits ([expr.const.core] paragraph 2.7). An infinite chain through specializations has the undefined result [temp.inst] paragraph 18 gives any infinite template recursion. The design raises no termination question that a `consteval` block or a template does not already raise.

## "The template scheduling rule is the hardest part to specify" ## {#objections-scheduling}

Accepted. [[#timing-templates|The specialization rules]] are the part of this design with no precedent in the annotation wording, since [meta.reflection.annotation] gathers annotations per specialization and runs nothing. What bounds the cost is that the design fixes three rules and derives each from wording that exists: the uniqueness of each instantiated annotation under [dcl.attr.annotation] paragraph 3, the point of instantiation form of [temp.point] paragraph 1, and the nesting of a `consteval` block in an instantiated class ([[#timing-templates-core|what is fixed and what is left]]). The dependent contexts of [temp.point] and the ODR integration are named as open rather than resolved. The alternative that removes per-specialization scheduling, one callback on the template definition, observes the template rather than any specialization, so a rule about a dependent type has nothing to test, and [[#overview-callback|this design does not supply it]].

## "Granting the injection request turns an annotation into a supply-chain hazard" ## {#objections-injection}

An annotation [[#semantics-detection|opts in]] through its type, with nothing at the annotation site to mark it. Writing `[[=Foo{}]]` on any construct authorizes whatever `Foo`'s author put in its invocation. Under poll 2 alone that includes reaching an injection into the translation unit that wrote the annotation through a `consteval` block of a specialization the invocation instantiates, keyed on the block's position ([[#semantics-injection-cost|the cost of injection]]). If [expr.const.core] paragraph 2.32 is also widened, the invocation can inject directly, keyed on the annotation's own position. There is no spelling that applies the metadata while declining the behavior.

The hazard is real but bounded. An invocation runs under the ordinary constant-evaluation rules so it cannot reach the file system, the network, or any state the translation does not already own. Where it may inject is keyed on a position in the consumer's own source, the block's or the annotation's ([[#semantics-contract|the invocation contract]]).

Header-supplied templates and the `consteval` blocks ([[P3289R1]]) inside them already run in a consumer's translation unit with the consumer's types. With `access_context::unchecked()`, such code can already inspect private members once it holds a reflection ([[P2996R13]]). What an active annotation adds is the trigger, which runs a header's code without the consumer calling anything, but only where a consumer wrote the annotation and only when the annotation's type was written for this protocol ([[#semantics-detection|the opt-in rule]]).

# Related work stays outside this proposal # {#future}

None of the following is a prerequisite for this proposal:

* schema declarations or markers
* named surface syntax for library-defined metadata
* editing annotation metadata before it is read, whose motivating uses (a field name defaulting to the member's identifier, a prefix applied across a class, markers folded into one value, and a count over members) are read-time derivations through an accessor the annotation's library ships
* [[#shape-mutation|changes to the annotated construct]] itself
* declaration-side constraints on accepted annotation types
* emission-record or runtime-transport reflection facilities
* relaxation of the structural-type restriction

An invocation runs during constant evaluation and can call any code-injection facility that exists, such as token sequences ([[P3294R2]]). We provide the invocation and leave the operations it can perform to those proposals.

# Policy choices and Core drafting remain open # {#open-questions}

Each question below turns on a policy or a preference.

* Opt-in policy. Whether direct-call validity should replace the declared parameter is [[#polls|poll 1]].
* Callee forms. Whether conversion functions and surrogate call functions stay in the opt-in, at the cost of a cross-reference to [over.call.object] paragraph 2 and one widening, or the rule is narrowed to call operators and pointers to functions, losing only the `ViaPointer` form ([[#semantics-detection|the opt-in rule]]).
* Complete-class contexts. What order is required when an invocation needs an in-class function body or initializer and when such a body needs a declaration an invocation injected? No position is offered here ([[#timing|processing points]]).
* Mutation and self-definition. Which uses would justify an invocation changing the construct it applies to? Should the ban on an invocation defining its own target remain ([[#shape-mutation|mutation]], [[#semantics-contract|the invocation contract]])?
* Context surface. Are member accessors, non-structural literal-type semantics, and the specified copy behavior appropriate? Does retaining a context in a later constant evaluation need further restriction? This is LEWG's to settle against the stated preference ([[#interface|the interface]]).

Core work identified here, rather than a design choice left open, covers whether the processing points and their order can be specified without unintended effects on constraint satisfaction and how the dependent contexts of [temp.point] paragraphs 1 and 4 are handled. [[#wording-impact|The wording table]] lists it.

# The polls ask about the opt-in, the protocol, and injection # {#polls}

Poll 1 is a preference. Poll 2 reads as the declared-parameter opt-in unless poll 1 carries. Poll 3 assumes poll 2.

1. EWG prefers that an annotation opt in by the validity of a direct call alone over requiring a callee that declares a `std::meta::annotation_context` parameter ([[#semantics-detection|the opt-in rule]]).
2. EWG encourages further work on an annotation invocation protocol in which an annotation that opts in, through a callee that declares a `std::meta::annotation_context` parameter or through the validity of a direct call if poll 1 carries, is invoked for each construct it applies to, a declaration or a base-specifier, with a `std::meta::annotation_context` describing that construct, in a mandatory constant evaluation whose failure is an error ([[#semantics|semantics]]).
3. EWG encourages further work on admitting the invocation of an active annotation under [expr.const.core] paragraph 2.32 so that it may produce an injected declaration where a `consteval` block at the annotation's position could ([[#semantics-contract|the invocation contract]]).

SG7 may take the polls first. The library surface (`annotation_context`) goes to LEWG once poll 2 has set the direction.

# Conclusion # {#conclusion}

The rule an annotation enforces is written once, in the annotation's type. Applying the annotation is enough to invoke it, with [[#alternatives-consteval-block|nothing for the class author to remember]]. The type of the annotation's value decides whether it [[#semantics-detection|opts in]], a decision that reads declarations only and instantiates no function body. Each occurrence/target pair not processed nested has its own processing point, in an order stated for each class and declaration. That is what makes a declaration injected by one annotation visible at a later annotation's point ([[#timing-order|lexical order]]).

C++ gains a checked annotation site. `serial::Field` rejects a member function at the member function ([[#example-serial|the field example]]). `GuardedBy` confirms that the mutex it names is a member of the same class wherever that mutex is declared ([[#example-thread|the guard example]]). `PrimaryKey` reports one diagnostic, at the second key ([[#example-multiplicity|the primary-key example]]). Without the facility the violation is reported away from the construct that caused it ([[#motivation-practice|existing practice]]).

No implementation is reported here and scheduling is the principal risk ([[#implementability|implementability]]). No wording is provided and the processing points and their order need [[#wording-impact|Core review]] ([[#open-questions|open questions]]). An injected member's annotation runs inside the injecting evaluation as a required constant expression of its own ([[#timing-injected|injected members]]). An infinite chain through [[#timing-templates|specializations]] has an undefined result, like any infinite template recursion. Where a codebase owns its declarations, a `consteval` block [[#alternatives-consteval-block|already runs the same checks]]. Every C++26 annotation keeps its behavior ([[#semantics-passive|compatibility]]).

The ask is [[#polls|the polls]]. Poll 1 is a preference between the declared parameter and direct-call validity. Its outcome sets how poll 2 reads. Poll 2 asks for direction on the invocation protocol. Poll 3 asks for direction on admitting injection and assumes poll 2. Declining poll 3 removes reflection-driven synthesis ([[#example-synthesis|the synthesis example]]) and the one rule that exists only because an invocation can inject directly ([[#semantics-injection-cost|the cost of injection]]).

A token-sequence injection facility ([[P3294R2]]) would give an invocation something to generate with, `define_enum` ([[P4033R1]]) would extend injected-annotation processing to enumerators, and a template-definition callback, which this design does not supply, is what a library composing metadata across a whole template would need.

# Disclosure # {#disclosure}

This is an individual contribution with no institutional affiliation. The author maintains no library that would consume this facility and has no current stake in its adoption, but anticipates using it in future work.

The paper asks for [[#polls|the polls]].

The comparisons drawn against the nearby papers are the author's own. [[P3385R8]] proposes attribute reflection, which this paper argues is the further route ([[#motivation-annotations-first|the motivation]]). [[P4167R0]] has the language read an annotation directly, which this paper treats as [[#alternatives-language-read|complementary]]. [[P3289R1]] supplies the `consteval` block, which this paper treats as [[#alternatives-consteval-block|the strongest argument against itself]]. [[P3294R2]] proposes code injection facilities an invocation could call. The author is an author of none of them.

The principal limitation of the method is that the paper identifies wording work it does not perform. Whether the processing points can be specified without unintended effects is classed as Core drafting ([[#open-questions|open questions]]) and is not demonstrated. The judgment that this is Core drafting rather than an unresolved design gap is the author's own and has not been put to Core.

Stable names and paragraph numbers were checked on 2026-09-23 against [[N5054]], using its PDF text and the [draft sources](https://github.com/cplusplus/draft) at the n5054 tag. Comparing the draft sources at that tag with those of that date shows no paragraph or list item added to or removed from any cited subclause. The numbers are therefore also those of the current draft ([[CPPDRAFT]]). Bibliography entries were checked against each document's own front matter rather than against an index. The absence claims were re-run on 2026-09-23. Reading each subclause in full shows that [cpp.predefined] carries no annotation-specific feature-test macro and that `<meta>` does not appear in the freestanding header table in [compliance]. [[P3394R4]] proposes no such macro. Neither `annotation_context` nor the names [[P2758R5]] proposes occur in the PDF text of [[N5054]] or in its draft sources. The [[#motivation-practice|existing-practice claims]] were checked on 2026-09-22 against each vendor's own documentation, which the bibliography records with that retrieval date.

Every quotation and every standard citation was checked against its primary source on 2026-09-23. That check covers what a source says and not whether this paper characterizes it correctly, which is the author's judgment throughout.

# Acknowledgments # {#acknowledgments}

The author thanks the Together C & C++ Discord community for reviewing drafts of this paper.

